{
  "$comment": "Single source of truth for material identity, shared by JS and C++. Edit here, then run `npm run generate` to regenerate materials.generated.js and cpp/engine/materials.generated.hpp. Numeric ids MUST stay stable: saved scenes, worldgen, tests, and the WASM grid key off them. `materialClass` is the broad gameplay/physics bucket and `flags` add cross-cutting traits, so a new material that reuses existing physics needs only a schema entry.",
  "tableSize": 64,
  "kinds": {
    "NONE": 0,
    "POWDER": 1,
    "LIQUID": 2,
    "GAS": 3,
    "COMPONENT": 4,
    "FREE_RIGID": 5
  },
  "$materialClassDoc": "Broad gameplay/physics class. Material ids remain exact identity; class answers high-level questions like gas/liquid/loose solid/rigid.",
  "materialClasses": {
    "none": 0,
    "gas": 1,
    "solid": 2,
    "rigid": 3,
    "liquid": 4
  },
  "$renderAnimDoc": "Render-only texture motion. Numeric ids are generated into C++ so the renderer, refresh policy, and palette share this schema field without hardcoded material checks.",
  "renderAnims": {
    "none": 0,
    "fire": 1,
    "steam": 2,
    "smoke": 3,
    "water": 4,
    "oil": 5,
    "acid": 6,
    "lava": 7,
    "methane": 8
  },
  "$flagDoc": "Bit index per behavior flag. flammable: ignites near fire/lava. dissolvable: acid eats it. rigid: a solid that grounds via rigid contact + is moved by moveRigidAssemblies. bearing: supports loose material above it (rigid ∪ sand-like powders). plantFamily: belongs to the growable plant-component family (flooded unbounded; fire-split).",
  "flagBits": {
    "flammable": 0,
    "dissolvable": 1,
    "rigid": 2,
    "bearing": 3,
    "plantFamily": 4,
    "quenchesLava": 5,
    "relaxesGaps": 6,
    "plantWood": 7,
    "plantLeaf": 8
  },
  "$toolDoc": "Mining gate (survival): toolClass is the preferred mining class and the drop gate for specialized tools. A destroyed cell drops an item if the material is hand-tier, or if the held tool's class matches toolClass AND its tier >= toolTier. The dig tool is a universal digger: it matches every material class for drops (still tier-gated) and digs at a flat speed so only DURABILITY (hardness) matters. toolClass 'none' never drops (liquids/gas/free-rigid). Wrong class or too-low tier still breaks the block, it just yields nothing.",
  "toolClasses": {
    "none": 0,
    "pickaxe": 1,
    "axe": 2,
    "shovel": 3,
    "hand": 4,
    "dig": 5
  },
  "toolTiers": {
    "hand": 0,
    "wood": 1,
    "stone": 2,
    "iron": 3,
    "gold": 4
  },
  "$miningSpeedDoc": "Mining progress uses 16 fixed-point units per baseline hit. Rows in classPercent are held tool classes and columns are the material's preferred toolClass, both in numeric enum order. tierPercent is indexed by held tool tier. Effective power scales by both percentages and progressDivisor; DURABILITY remains the base hardness. The dig row is flat so dig speed depends only on hardness (+ tool tier). Survival applies progress every held tick.",
